  "license": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/",
  "url": "https://huggingface.co/datasets/gt-free-ocr-metrics/omnidocbench-render-compare-parquet",
  "rai:dataLimitations": "Coverage is restricted to the 1,355 pages of OmniDocBench (English and Chinese only); results should not be generalised to other languages, scripts, or document types not present in OmniDocBench. Because the OCR model both classifies and extracts document elements, misclassification errors (e.g. a formula region tagged as text) propagate silently: both the masked original and the reconstruction erase the misclassified region consistently, so visual similarity remains artificially high while reference-based metrics correctly score low. Bounding-box detection by Qwen3.5-122B-A10B is not always perfect, particularly on pages which appear to be manually scanned or pages where there is very high information density. The ocr_formula subset covers only 200 pages where the OCR model detected at least one formula; the ocr_table subset covers only 351 such pages. Reconstructions are drawn element by element on a white canvas at the original page resolution; font substitution, approximate font sizes and rendering artefacts relative to the original scans are present throughout.",
  "rai:dataBiases": "The underlying OmniDocBench covers nine document categories with the following page counts (from its page_attribute.data_source field) across the 1,355 pages: slide presentations (PPT2PDF) 241 pages, exam papers 184 pages, books 176 pages, newspapers 151 pages, magazines 139 pages, colourful textbooks 138 pages, academic papers 129 pages, notes 116 pages, and research reports 81 pages. By language, 632 pages are Chinese, 628 English and 95 mixed English and Chinese. The DocSim triplet training set (docsim_triplets config, 20,280 triplets) is derived from the same 1,355-page pool and inherits the same distribution.",
  "rai:personalSensitiveInformation": "No personally identifiable information (PII) was intentionally collected. OmniDocBench draws from public academic and professional documents. Financial report pages may incidentally reference company names or executive names. No medical, legal, biometric, or demographic data is present. Applicable sensitive categories: none intentionally present. No de-identification steps were applied beyond what is already present in the source OmniDocBench dataset.",
  "rai:dataUseCases": "Intended uses: (1) research on reference-free OCR evaluation using visual similarity between original page scans and rendered HTML reconstructions, without requiring ground-truth text annotations; (2) development and evaluation of perceptual document similarity metrics across diverse real-world layouts in English and Chinese; (3) training document similarity models using the DocSim triplet manifold (docsim_triplets config, 20,280 triplets with Hungarian-matched edit-distance supervision). Validity of the visual similarity signal as a proxy for OCR quality has been examined for correlation with edit distance (text and formulas) and TEDS (tables) on OmniDocBench pages. Out-of-scope uses: extracting or reproducing copyrighted document text from reconstructed images or OCR HTML outputs; using this as a ground-truth OCR corpus (reconstructed content is model output, not a verified transcript); training general-purpose OCR models; clinical, legal, or safety-critical applications.",
  "rai:dataSocialImpact": "Positive impacts: enables reference-free OCR quality assessment without requiring annotated ground truth, reducing barriers to OCR evaluation in resource-constrained settings. The DocSim triplets enable training of lightweight document similarity heads for downstream evaluation tooling. Potential risks: visual similarity metrics developed on this dataset may be misapplied beyond document OCR evaluation. Reconstructed images and OCR HTML outputs must not be used to extract or redistribute copyrighted text. The CC-BY-NC-4.0 license restricts commercial use.",
  "rai:hasSyntheticData": true,
